NXR-161|Profile RAM reconciliation|The release gate shall reconcile every allocated RAM section and boot-reserved stack against the declared board memory map.|Sum all disjoint load and runtime ranges; reject an omitted boot stack and any overlap or address overflow.|Compare linker map and ELF program headers with a separately authored board manifest; inject each omission.|Zero unexplained RAM bytes; board capacity is a hard ceiling.|Current static allocation contracts do not establish a complete per-profile board reconciliation.
NXR-162|Allocation gate detection controls|The allocator audit shall prove detection of each forbidden allocator family using deliberately linked negative fixtures.|Fixtures referencing malloc, new, aligned allocation and allocator wrappers fail; a heap-free control passes.|Build positive and negative ELF fixtures with the same release flags and linker garbage collection.|Zero permitted allocator references; fixtures remain host build artifacts.|A passing symbol scan alone cannot show that archive extraction and renamed wrappers are detected.
NXR-163|Critical section measurement coverage|The qualification report shall enumerate every interrupt-masked path and its input-dependent work bound.|Queue maximum copies, waiter scans, donation chains and trace operations each have a source path and measured bound.|Instrument target mask entry and exit; test minimum and maximum configured capacities and calibrated probe overhead.|Per-profile maximum mask duration is declared before measurement; no unbounded callback under mask.|The kernel uses local interrupt serialization, so latency claims need coverage of all masked work.
NXR-164|Stack evidence completeness|The stack report shall distinguish untouched sentinel bytes from proven unused call-chain space.|A deliberately deep call and an interrupt at its deepest point are detected; incomplete scanning is reported as incomplete.|Combine compiler stack usage, call-chain review and target watermark fixtures including boot and fault paths.|Every enabled stack has a byte ceiling including nested exception frames.|Watermarks are observations and cannot independently bound unexecuted paths.
NXR-165|Feature removal differential|The release gate shall compare disabled-feature firmware against enabled firmware using identical toolchains and application behavior.|Disabled DMA, logging and tracing remove their symbols and reserved pools; a hidden residual pool fails.|Retain map diffs and ELF symbol checks for one toggle at a time.|Disabled feature contributes zero feature-owned RAM and code beyond documented shared dependencies.|Optional source compilation does not guarantee that associated static storage disappears.
NXR-166|Timing units provenance|Every timing result shall carry target frequency, counter width, conversion rule and measurement origin.|Changing frequency without regenerating the profile rejects qualification; host seconds cannot populate target-time fields.|Exercise two clock profiles and counter wrap with known interval fixtures.|Conversions use checked arithmetic and a declared rounding bound of at most one target tick.|Host elapsed time and simulated timer advancement were previously easy to confuse.
NXR-167|Capacity sweep qualification|The qualification gate shall exercise legal minimum, representative and maximum resource capacities.|Thread, queue and waiter boundaries reject excess operations without mutation at every selected capacity.|Run independent reference models and target boundary fixtures; retain configuration identity per case.|No capacity-dependent allocation; total execution budget declared for each sweep.|Testing one default capacity misses index and fairness boundary defects.
NXR-168|Idle residency accounting|CPU residency reports shall identify measured busy and idle intervals without counting blocked host simulation time as target execution.|A controlled idle-heavy workload and busy loop produce ordered residency results; totals reconcile within declared sampling error.|Compare target counter accounting with external GPIO intervals and explicit sampling overhead.|Accounting work is constant per transition; error ceiling declared in target counter units.|Existing metrics need a calibrated interpretation before they support performance claims.
NXR-169|Reproducible optimized builds|Qualified firmware and interpreter builds shall record effective compiler options and require the designated optimized profile to use -O3.|A substituted -O2 interpreter or wrong language standard rejects the optimized qualification label.|Inspect compilation databases and embedded build identity; execute a wrong-flags negative fixture.|C++20 interpreter; -O3 release profile; debug and sanitizer profiles remain separately named.|Requested optimization must be verified from effective commands rather than preset names.
NXR-170|Performance regression envelope|The performance gate shall compare fixed workloads only under matching hardware, compiler, features and measurement methods.|Changed profile identities prevent direct speedup claims; a seeded slow-path injection crosses and fails the recorded threshold.|Retain baseline distributions, repetitions, machine identity and confidence method alongside raw observations.|Per-workload threshold and repeat count fixed before candidate execution.|An observed speed difference is meaningless when build or measurement conditions change.
NXR-171|Optional lifecycle compatibility|A future lifecycle profile shall use boot-reserved thread slots while preserving sealed registration and permanent completion in the default profile.|Default post-start create still fails; lifecycle-disabled binaries expose no runtime recycling path.|Compile both profiles and execute default registration regressions plus opt-in slot state fixtures.|Fixed slot and stack pool; zero general heap; lifecycle remains opt-in.|Runtime lifetime support need not imply dynamic allocation but must not silently alter the current contract.
NXR-172|Thread generation exhaustion|A recyclable thread handle shall contain a generation that never wraps into a previously valid handle during a boot.|An old handle fails after slot reuse; the final representable generation exhausts the slot rather than wrapping.|Use a reduced-width generation fixture and validate real-width boundary arithmetic.|One generation word per reserved slot; exhausted slots remain unavailable until reset.|Index-only handles would allow stale commands to control a different thread incarnation.
NXR-173|Cooperative stop acknowledgement|A stop request shall be acknowledged as complete only after the worker reaches a documented safe point and settles owned resources.|A worker retaining a mutex or DMA ownership cannot report stopped; a safe worker acknowledges once.|Pause workers at each ownership transition and issue duplicate stop requests.|One stop flag and acknowledgement per permanent worker; finite grace deadline.|Setting a stop flag is not evidence that a worker is safe to reuse.
NXR-174|Thread join evidence|An optional join operation shall distinguish running, completed, timed-out and stale generations without consuming another incarnation's completion.|Two joiners observe the documented policy; timeout and completion races never return an unrelated generation.|Enumerate completion-before-wait, wait-before-completion and timeout boundaries in a state model.|Fixed join waiter capacity; no allocation and no infinite cleanup scan.|The current finished state is not a join or storage-reclamation protocol.
NXR-175|Thread reclamation barrier|A lifecycle slot shall become reusable only after all scheduler, waiter, observer and ownership references to its old generation are retired.|Attempt reuse with one stale waiter or pending observer reference and receive a bounded refusal.|Inject each retained reference separately and verify no stack or control-block overwrite.|Reference metadata bounded by configured threads and waiters; no runtime stack growth.|Thread completion alone cannot prove that all kernel references are gone.
NXR-176|Abandoned mutex policy|The lifecycle API shall reject normal reclamation of a thread that still owns a mutex and report the ownership violation.|Exit or stop with one and multiple owned mutexes produces the declared fault without silently transferring protected data.|Exercise nested ownership and inherited priority chains with an independent ownership ledger.|No forced unlock; reporting bounded by configured owned-mutex metadata.|Releasing a dead owner's lock can expose partially updated application state.
NXR-177|Cancellation wait removal|An optional cancellation path shall remove exactly the cancelled generation's pending wait under the same serialization as grant and timeout.|Grant, timeout and cancel permutations yield one terminal result and conserve queue items and semaphore tokens.|Exhaustively model finite interleavings and run seeded target races.|At most configured waiter scans; no callback or device access inside the wait lock.|A second terminal path can duplicate grants unless it shares the existing wait arbitration.
NXR-178|Lifecycle priority cleanup|Slot reuse shall discard inherited priority and donation links from the retired generation before publishing the new thread.|A reused low-priority thread cannot inherit a prior owner's donated priority or remain in its chain.|Build chained donation fixtures and reuse each retired slot position.|Cleanup bounded by configured mutex and thread counts.|Donation metadata belongs to an incarnation rather than a reusable storage address.
NXR-179|Worker activation conservation|Permanent-worker test jobs shall use unique run and job identities and reject activation while the prior job is unsettled.|Duplicate activation executes once; a new run cannot consume a queued old-run command.|Use a bounded command queue with replayed commands and delayed acknowledgements.|One active job per worker and a fixed outstanding command window.|Boot-created workers already support repeated jobs without promising runtime thread creation.
NXR-180|Lifecycle fault recovery boundary|Lifecycle failure shall distinguish recoverable quiescence from a reset-required ownership domain.|A cooperative timeout with unsettled DMA reports reset required and never permits slot or buffer reuse.|Inject failed stop and incomplete resource release and verify the reset policy against observed accesses.|No automatic force-delete; finite stop deadline and retained first failure.|A cleanup timeout does not establish that hardware or kernel references have stopped.
NXR-181|Independent RISC-V feature matrix|Qualification shall build baseline, Zaamo-only, M/U-PMP-only and combined profiles as four independent configurations.|Each profile reports its actual ISA and privilege capability; disabling either extension preserves baseline behavior.|Compile and execute profile-specific positive and illegal-operation fixtures.|Single hart; fixed four-region PMP profile where enabled; no implicit MMU.|Atomics and privilege protection are separate axes and should not become one coupled feature.
NXR-182|Zaamo capability mismatch|The platform shall reject an atomic-capable build when the selected execution target or RAM window lacks the required Zaamo contract.|Wrong-capability manifests fail before workload release; baseline fallback uses its explicitly selected implementation.|Run manifest mismatch controls and RAM versus MMIO atomic address fixtures.|No atomics on device memory; no silent runtime emulation inside the hardware qualification profile.|Instruction availability alone does not establish atomic behavior for every mapped address.
NXR-183|Atomic return-value oracle|Zaamo qualification shall check both stored values and returned old values for every supported operation.|Exchange, add, OR and AND return and store the expected words; signed min and max are tested only when separately advertised by the instruction model.|Execute edge operands and randomized word pairs; mutate return-value handling to prove detection.|Fixed operand corpus plus declared seed and operation count; no heap on target.|Final memory checks can miss an incorrect register result from an atomic instruction.
NXR-184|Atomic ordering witnesses|Atomic memory ordering claims shall be limited to the declared single-hart interrupt and device-ordering model.|Release-acquire publication under preemption succeeds; unsupported multicore ordering is reported unsupported.|Run interrupt handoff litmus tests and inspect emitted fences with the selected compiler.|No SMP qualification claim; bounded shared ordinary-RAM words.|A single-hart stress pass cannot establish multicore memory-order semantics.
NXR-185|User entry register sanitation|An optional U-mode entry shall initialize privilege state and all exposed register contents according to a documented ABI.|Entry cannot inherit privileged stack pointers or stale kernel data; legal user entry reaches its declared PC.|Use register-pattern fixtures and inspect mstatus, mepc and return privilege on an independent execution model.|One declared user frame per slot; no FP or vector state unless separately enabled.|Processor U-mode support does not by itself supply a safe operating-system user boundary.
NXR-186|PMP boundary access matrix|Four-region PMP qualification shall test the last allowed and first forbidden byte for every configured access permission.|Read, write and execute violations trap with the expected cause and address; permitted boundary accesses succeed.|Generate byte, halfword and word probes including region-crossing accesses on RTL and interpreter.|Exactly four declared regions; reject unrepresentable layouts before enabling user execution.|Region setup needs boundary evidence rather than only checking CSR readback.
NXR-187|PMP lock semantics|PMP lock qualification shall verify the documented reset-only unlock behavior and machine-mode access effects.|Writes to locked configuration fail to change it; accesses obey the selected locked permissions until reset.|Attempt configuration and address rewrites and compare reset and pre-reset behavior.|No runtime unlock API; fixed board reset domain.|A simulated region table can miss architectural lock effects and their reset lifetime.
NXR-188|Trap origin integrity|Trap dispatch shall preserve and report the originating privilege mode without allowing user-controlled state to impersonate kernel origin.|User ecall and illegal CSR access are classified correctly; nested fault fixtures retain original context evidence.|Compare saved status and cause against independently calculated trap transitions.|Fixed trap frames; bounded nesting policy explicitly declared.|Shared trap entry must distinguish a legitimate user request from a kernel exception.
NXR-189|Interpreter timer service modes|The interpreter shall separate instruction-exact timer delivery from opt-in batched delivery and report the configured delay bound.|Batch size one agrees with exact delivery; batch size ten never exceeds its declared instruction delay in eligible execution.|Use known-deadline instruction streams, branch loops and masked-interrupt cases with reference traces.|Batch parameter finite and recorded; no timing-accuracy label for relaxed delivery.|Reducing timer checks can improve throughput while changing interrupt placement and must be visible.
NXR-190|Interpreter RTL differential trace|Instruction-model qualification shall compare architectural retire state with RTL for matched finite workloads.|Register, PC, trap cause and touched memory first diverge at a recorded instruction identity; an injected opcode defect is detected.|Run short deterministic instruction, atomic and privilege corpora with identical reset state.|Bounded trace window and operation count; model speed does not imply cycle accuracy.|Fast interpretation needs an independent oracle rather than self-comparison of final signatures.
NXR-191|Random stream versioning|Randomized tests shall identify the generator algorithm and version as well as the seed and stream identifier.|A golden vector reproduces across host and target builds; changing the algorithm without changing version fails.|Publish fixed seed vectors including zero and maximal seeds and compare independent implementations.|Fixed generator state per worker; no entropy dependency for replay.|A seed alone cannot reproduce a test after the generator or stream partition changes.
NXR-192|Schedule choice recording|Replay reports shall distinguish deterministic workload inputs from nondeterministic interrupt and scheduling choices.|Identical seeds with different timer delivery produce separate schedule identities; exact replay is claimed only with sufficient recorded choices.|Repeat workloads with shifted interrupt points and compare input and schedule logs separately.|Fixed trace ring with explicit loss counters; loss disables exact replay claims.|Seeded jobs are reproducible even when their legal thread interleavings are not.
NXR-193|Job oracle independence|Each randomized job family shall have a reference calculation that does not reuse the tested implementation path.|A deliberate error in arithmetic, queue ordering or payload generation yields a mismatch even with a valid transport CRC.|Use separately authored reference functions and mutation fixtures for each job family.|Fixed input and result sizes; bounded outstanding jobs.|A checksum of the implementation's own answer is not an independent correctness oracle.
NXR-194|Replay bundle completeness|A replay bundle shall include firmware, source revision, dirty diff, tool versions, configuration, seed and the terminal classification.|Removing any mandatory identity makes the bundle incomplete; a clean and dirty build remain distinguishable.|Validate bundle schema against intentionally missing fields and hash mismatches.|Host storage quota declared per run; archive exhaustion cannot be reported as a pass.|A commit ID cannot identify uncommitted source or substituted firmware bytes.
NXR-195|First-fault retention scope|Fault reports shall state whether their storage survives only the current boot or a qualified reset domain.|Ordinary RAM is never labeled reset-retained; a later fault cannot overwrite the first fault in the same capture epoch.|Trigger two distinct faults and then each supported reset kind; compare retained identity and validity markers.|One fixed first-fault record per capture epoch; no allocation in capture.|The current FirstFault helper explicitly does not provide reset or double-fault retention.
NXR-196|Trace overflow semantics|Trace consumers shall report lost events and forbid complete-history conclusions after overflow.|Fill the ring, drop events, drain and refill; loss count is visible and saturates without wrapping.|Exercise capacity one, full and counter-boundary fixtures and malformed consumer sequence claims.|Configured event capacity only; overflow never blocks producers.|A bounded trace is useful evidence only when omitted history remains explicit.
NXR-197|Failure minimization preservation|A failure reducer shall preserve the original bundle and emit every reduced candidate as a separately identified artifact.|A reduced workload that stops reproducing is rejected as a reproducer; original inputs remain immutable.|Replay a seeded injected failure, remove operations and verify stable failure identity.|Finite reduction attempts and host time budget; no autonomous unbounded search.|Simplifying a randomized failure can accidentally remove the trigger while retaining a similar symptom.
NXR-198|Mismatch first witness|A result mismatch shall retain the first worker, job sequence, operation and expected versus observed value before summarizing signatures.|Two mismatches preserve the earliest witness; report truncation cannot erase its identity.|Inject a known bad job followed by a second fault and saturate normal telemetry.|One fixed witness record plus bounded context window.|Aggregate mismatch counts do not identify the operation needed to reproduce a defect.
NXR-199|Endurance completion criteria|An endurance result shall require its declared target operations and target duration independently of host cutoff.|A host deadline reached before target goals reports wall limit or incomplete rather than passed.|Run deliberately slow simulation with short host cutoff and a separate completed control.|Finite host cutoff plus cleanup allowance; target goals recorded before start.|A twelve-hour host window does not imply twelve hours of simulated target execution.
NXR-200|Detection qualification campaign|Each claimed invariant checker shall detect at least one controlled violation of its own property.|Disabled or always-successful checkers fail qualification; every injected violation has an expected classification.|Create explicit controls for context corruption, duplicate jobs, stale handles and bad UART content.|Fault injection confined to named test profiles; no release default activation.|Healthy runs alone cannot demonstrate that diagnostic checks discriminate failures.
NXR-201|Fixed-frame wire schema|The HIL codec shall encode the documented 512-byte frame using explicit little-endian fields and zero padding.|Golden frames match byte for byte on host and target; native struct packing changes do not alter encoding.|Cross-check independent encoders and a published offset table including all boundary fields.|512 decoded bytes: 40 header, 468 payload region and 4 CRC bytes.|The wire proposal exists but no frozen executable codec currently establishes compatibility.
NXR-202|CRC coverage controls|The codec shall validate CRC-32C over all first 508 decoded bytes including padding before dispatch.|Single-bit changes in header, payload and padding fail; the standard check string yields E3069283.|Use independent bitwise and optimized implementations plus fixed known-answer frames.|Four-byte little-endian CRC; polynomial 82F63B78 with declared initial and final XOR.|Validating only meaningful payload would leave framing metadata and padding unchecked.
NXR-203|COBS resynchronization bounds|The decoder shall bound encoded accumulation and recover at a delimiter after malformed or oversized input.|All-zero payload and maximal nonzero runs round-trip; oversized or truncated frames never grow storage or dispatch commands.|Feed arbitrary read fragments, consecutive delimiters and corrupted code bytes followed by a valid frame.|At most 516 encoded bytes including delimiter; statically reserved 576-byte slots.|USB and serial read boundaries do not correspond to logical frame boundaries.
NXR-204|Packet rejection ordering|Packet validation shall reject magic, version, reserved fields, length and CRC errors before command side effects.|Each malformed field independently causes rejection and leaves run state, output ownership and command cache unchanged.|Compare state snapshots around one-field mutations and wrong-endian fixtures.|Constant frame ceiling; no payload-dependent allocation or callback before validation.|A checksum pass is insufficient to authorize a structurally invalid command.
NXR-205|Session and boot identity binding|Every state-changing command shall match the negotiated session nonce and current boot identity.|A captured pre-reset command and a command from another host session cannot start or stop work.|Replay frames across reconnection and controlled resets with unchanged command numbers.|128-bit session nonce and 64-bit boot identity per wire proposal; entropy quality stated separately.|Sequence numbers alone cannot isolate sessions across reboot and USB enumeration.
NXR-206|Command retry idempotency|The target shall return a cached response for an exact retry of the last command without repeating side effects.|Duplicate START and STOP execute once; reuse of the same command ID with different bytes is rejected.|Drop the first response, resend identical and modified requests and count actual state transitions.|One command in flight; one bounded request-response cache.|Transport retries need execution identity rather than blind re-dispatch of commands.
NXR-207|Transport sequence interpretation|The receiver shall distinguish transport loss and duplicate frames from logical report or command identity.|A repeated fault report with a new transport sequence is not a new fault; missing transport sequence increments loss evidence.|Inject reordered, duplicate and wrapping transport sequences with stable logical identities.|Fixed tracking window and documented sequence wrap comparison.|Transport sequencing and logical event deduplication solve different problems.
NXR-208|Status paging coherence|Paged status shall carry a sample epoch and page bounds so a host never merges incompatible snapshots.|More than eight workers are reported over pages; a changed epoch during collection invalidates the aggregate.|Mutate worker counters between pages and test missing, duplicate and out-of-range pages.|Eight 32-byte thread slots per status frame; bounded page count declared by profile.|A single 512-byte packet cannot hold an unbounded worker inventory.
NXR-209|Capability validity encoding|Optional telemetry fields shall carry explicit validity and capability metadata rather than treating zero as unsupported.|A supported zero-error ECC counter differs from absent ECC hardware; unsupported latency does not display as zero latency.|Round-trip absent, valid-zero, saturated and stale metrics in schema fixtures.|Validity bits and typed units fit the fixed schema; no implicit feature inference.|Zero measurements can otherwise create false claims about unavailable hardware features.
NXR-210|Protocol compatibility negotiation|Handshake shall reject incompatible major schemas and negotiate only documented compatible capabilities before CONFIGURE.|Unknown mandatory features and incompatible packet layout fail without arming workers; optional unknown fields follow the stated policy.|Run old/new schema fixture pairs and unsupported-command probes.|Bounded capability bitmap and finite handshake deadline.|Version numbers need an explicit compatibility rule before mixed host and firmware builds are safe.
NXR-211|Exclusive durable board lease|The host manager shall lease a target by durable board identity and reject a concurrent owner even if the serial port changes.|Swap enumeration, run a second manager and hotplug the board; identity remains stable and exclusivity holds.|Use fake serial endpoints plus two real processes contending for one configured board.|One bounded session per configured board; OS lease lifetime tied to owner process.|A COM port name is neither a durable target identity nor a sufficient ownership lock.
NXR-212|Independent session supervision|A blocked target shall not block progress collection, deadlines or cleanup for other targets.|Stall one reader and disconnect another while a third completes its declared run without missed deadlines.|Execute a three-endpoint scenario with bounded asynchronous cancellation and retained per-board logs.|Finite per-board queues and deadlines; no unbounded task creation.|A shared blocking serial loop would turn one board failure into a bench-wide loss of evidence.
NXR-213|Immutable start manifest|Before START the host shall freeze requested and effective firmware, board, checker, schema and workload identities.|Firmware hash substitution or mismatched capability rejects START; later configuration edits create a new run identity.|Tamper with each manifest component between configuration and arming.|One immutable manifest per run; checked file sizes and hashes.|Desired settings are not evidence that the target actually accepted those settings.
NXR-214|Bounded evidence writing|Evidence backpressure shall terminate or explicitly degrade the run without silently losing mandatory terminal records.|Fill the host storage quota and stall the writer; result becomes incomplete and first-fault retention remains accounted for.|Use a quota-limited sink and injected I/O failures with a healthy control run.|Declared bytes-per-run ceiling and bounded writer queue; no unlimited RAM buffering.|Durable evidence failures must be distinguished from a healthy target pass.
NXR-215|Per-board terminal classification|The host shall classify target failure, link loss, unresponsive target, stop timeout, host cutoff and incomplete evidence separately.|Each injected failure maps to exactly its documented classification; timeout alone cannot assert kernel failure.|Drive fake-device state transitions and physical unplug/reset cases.|One terminal result per run with append-only transition history.|Transport silence cannot reveal whether the kernel, cable or host reader failed.
NXR-216|Host restart recovery|After manager restart the host shall reconcile archived identity and live target state before issuing any state-changing command.|A live old run is attached as observed or stopped by explicit policy; it is never silently duplicated.|Kill the host after START and before terminal persistence, then restart with persisted manifest.|Bounded recovery handshake deadline and one active lease.|Process restart is a separate state transition from target reset.
NXR-217|Cancellation cleanup evidence|Host cancellation shall record stop request, acknowledgement, cleanup deadline and residual target state.|Cancel during handshake, running and result fetch; each phase preserves a truthful terminal outcome.|Use cancellation injection at every awaited operation and a target that refuses stop.|Finite cleanup allowance; no indefinite wait after host cutoff.|Returning from a cancelled task does not prove that the device stopped running.
NXR-218|Raw and decoded evidence linkage|Every decoded report shall retain a link to raw bytes and decoder version for later reanalysis.|A malformed frame remains available as raw evidence; changing decoder version produces a separate interpretation.|Replay an archived byte stream through two schema versions and compare stable raw hashes.|Raw capture quota declared; any discarded bytes have explicit counters.|JSON summaries alone cannot diagnose decoder defects or framing loss.
NXR-219|Safe test selection|The manager shall start only declared allowlisted test IDs and reject arbitrary address, register or memory-write requests.|Unknown IDs, out-of-range parameters and unsupported fault injection fail before START.|Fuzz command selection and validate that no raw debug command reaches the monitor.|Finite test registry and bounded parameter schema.|The HIL monitor is a test executive rather than an unrestricted remote debugger.
NXR-220|Host codec performance budget|The host shall measure throughput and allocation behavior under the declared maximum board count without starving supervision.|At configured board count and report rate all deadlines remain serviced; overload reports queue pressure explicitly.|Replay maximal frames from simulated endpoints and sample memory, CPU and per-board deadline delay.|Maximum board count, queue depth and host memory ceiling fixed before qualification.|Async code alone does not prove bounded resource use at bench scale.
NXR-221|Monitor legal transition table|The target executive shall enforce the documented command-state transition table without implicit starts.|CONFIGURE, ARM, START, STOP and REARM issued in every state either follow the table or fail unchanged.|Exhaustively exercise the finite state-command matrix including duplicate commands.|Fixed state enum and one active run; bounded command processing.|The proposed states need executable rejection tests as well as happy-path scenarios.
NXR-222|All-worker start barrier|RUNNING shall be reported only after every selected worker acknowledges the same run generation.|Delay or omit one worker acknowledgement; the monitor remains starting or fails its deadline without claiming full activity.|Gate workers individually and compare acknowledgements with externally observed activity.|One acknowledgement bit per configured worker and finite start deadline.|Dispatching START does not prove that all required workers became active.
NXR-223|Stop ownership reconciliation|COMPLETED shall require all selected workers quiescent and all test-owned resources reconciled.|A pending DMA transfer or unreturned queue descriptor prevents COMPLETED even if worker loops have ended.|Hold each resource type in turn and verify stop classification and residual inventory.|Finite reconciliation pass over declared test resources.|Worker inactivity is insufficient when hardware or descriptors still own buffers.
NXR-224|Rearm control-queue preservation|REARM shall reset only settled test resources and preserve permanent monitor control channels.|An idle control waiter survives REARM and accepts the next command; stale job descriptors are rejected.|Repeat configure-run-stop-rearm cycles with queued old-generation work and idle control waiters.|No blanket queue reset; fixed run-generation metadata.|Permanent control waiters are legitimate idle state and must not be drained as leaked test resources.
NXR-225|Fault state quiescence distinction|FAULTED shall report quiescence independently and forbid reuse when ownership remains uncertain.|Inject a worker fault during active DMA; fault evidence is retained while reuse remains blocked.|Model fault and stop transitions and inspect hardware ownership until reset.|Separate fault and quiescence fields; one retained first-fault slot.|A fault label is not proof that external activity has stopped.
NXR-226|Monitor noninterference budget|The monitor shall publish its priority, sampling cost and transport budget so test results account for observer load.|Compare workload progress with telemetry disabled and enabled; overhead stays within the predeclared envelope.|Measure target cycles and external GPIO timing under summary and trace modes.|Default summary rate one per second; fixed service budget per dispatch.|The test executive can perturb the scheduler behavior it is measuring.
NXR-227|Command descriptor ownership|Monitor queues shall carry bounded frame descriptors rather than copying full 512-byte packets under the kernel queue lock.|Largest command enqueue respects the 128-byte copy limit; descriptor ownership is conserved on reject and cancel.|Fill every pool slot and inject parse failures, duplicate responses and cancellation.|Static frame pool and descriptor queue; proposed protocol storage ceiling 6784 bytes excluding stacks and descriptors.|The fixed packet size exceeds the existing queue copy limit and needs separate frame storage.
NXR-228|Terminal evidence acknowledgement|The monitor shall retain final result and fault records until the host acknowledges the matching logical identity.|Drop result frames and reconnect; identical logical evidence remains fetchable and is not replaced by a new run.|Exercise lost acknowledgement, duplicate fetch and stale acknowledgement cases.|One retained terminal result and first fault; finite fetch payloads.|A transport write success does not establish durable host receipt.
NXR-229|Monitor parameter validation|CONFIGURE shall validate worker selection, job bounds, durations and resource needs before changing the active configuration.|Overflowing durations, zero illegal counts and excessive frame demand fail without partial resource acquisition.|Test each parameter boundary and a compound invalid configuration against state snapshots.|Checked arithmetic and declared fixed capacities; no allocation on configure.|Malformed test parameters can invalidate the resource guarantees of an otherwise bounded workload.
NXR-230|Sacrificial worker reboot policy|A test that permanently exits a boot-created worker shall declare the run non-rearmable without the required reset.|After exit the monitor reports reboot required and rejects another run needing that worker.|Run exit-focused fixtures and then attempt rearm and start with the same worker inventory.|No runtime thread resurrection in the default kernel profile.|Current exit semantics reserve storage and do not permit recreating finished workers.
NXR-231|Independent UART content oracle|The external checker shall validate expected per-worker content and sequence in addition to CRC and framing.|A wrong payload with a recomputed valid CRC fails; a valid multiworker stream passes.|Feed synthetic UART waveforms and physical target streams with independent expected payload generation.|Fixed per-worker oracle state and declared maximum worker count.|A valid CRC only proves consistency with the sender's bytes, not correct job execution.
NXR-232|Observer calibration identity|Every timing result shall identify checker clock, sampling resolution, synchronizer uncertainty and calibration validity.|Expired or mismatched calibration prevents a timing pass; known pulse widths fall within declared uncertainty.|Apply a reference pulse train at boundary widths and compare captured intervals.|Calibration version and finite validity interval recorded per checker.|An external clock is independent but still requires measurement uncertainty accounting.
NXR-233|UART framing fault sensitivity|UART checker qualification shall detect wrong baud, stop-bit violations, truncated records and FIFO overrun distinctly.|Inject each fault independently and preserve the first affected byte or record identity.|Generate controlled waveforms and throttle the checker input sink.|Declared baud tolerance, FIFO depth and maximum record length.|Successful text decoding does not qualify framing and overload error detection.
NXR-234|Physical latency witnesses|Latency tests shall measure stimulus arrival, handler entry and worker dispatch as separate physical events.|A delayed worker with prompt ISR entry is classified as scheduling delay rather than interrupt-entry delay.|Drive an external stimulus and capture three GPIO witnesses with calibrated timestamps.|Per-board limits declared before arming; GPIO overhead measured.|USB timestamps cannot separate interrupt latency from host transport latency.
NXR-235|Checker arming handshake|A target run requiring an observer shall start only after the matching checker run identity is armed and acknowledged.|Unarmed, stale or wrong-profile checker rejects the run; target and checker terminal identities must match.|Race checker reset and host START while validating a physical start marker.|One active checker run and finite arming deadline.|An independently running checker can still observe the wrong target run without identity binding.
NXR-236|Checker fault injection controls|Each observer detection channel shall have a controlled injection proving it can fail the run.|Disable a detector or alter a known-bad waveform; qualification detects the missing expected failure.|Run byte corruption, missing sequence, timing overrun and reset injections before acceptance tests.|Finite preflight corpus and explicit injection-enabled profile.|An FPGA signature accumulator can appear healthy while its comparison or reporting path is disabled.
NXR-237|Reset observation provenance|The bench shall distinguish host-requested reset, watchdog reset, power interruption and unexplained reboot using available independent evidence.|Controlled reset cases produce distinct evidence; unavailable reset-cause hardware yields unknown rather than a fabricated cause.|Correlate reset line, target boot identity and retained hardware reason where supported.|Bounded reset event history and declared reset-domain wiring.|A new boot counter alone cannot explain why the previous execution ended.
NXR-238|Supply and disconnect domain|Power interruption tests shall document which target, bridge and checker domains lose power and preserve evidence outside the tested domain.|Target-only power cycling does not erase checker evidence; shared-power loss is explicitly classified incomplete.|Execute the declared wiring matrix and verify reconnect identity and retained files.|Equipment current and voltage limits declared per board; no undeclared reset assumptions.|USB power wiring can reset the evidence collector along with the target.
NXR-239|RISCue hardware claim boundary|RISCue RTL, ECC and TMR hardware claims shall require the corresponding FPGA image and observable counters rather than commercial RISC-V silicon alone.|A commercial MCU run is accepted only for its HAL scope; absent ECC or TMR support cannot receive a hardware pass.|Validate image hashes, feature manifests and one controlled hardware event per claimed detector.|Declared FPGA image and exposed counter widths; unsupported checks skipped explicitly.|A compatible ISA does not implement the custom processor's redundancy or protection hardware.
NXR-240|Observer storage overflow result|A checker shall report capture loss and withhold complete-trace or timing-pass claims after overflow.|Exceed FIFO and capture depth; loss counters and terminal incomplete status appear while a bounded control passes.|Drive maximal-rate streams and delayed host drains at the declared input limit.|Fixed FIFO and capture RAM; no silent circular overwrite of mandatory first-fault evidence.|An apparently valid tail of captured output can hide dropped earlier failures.
NXR-241|UART whole-record atomicity|Queued UART qualification shall prove that concurrent producer records never interleave within a record across partial transport writes.|Three seeded producers emit counted records through one-byte writes; each complete record belongs to one producer and sequence.|Compare exact output with an independent record parser while forcing full and busy transport responses.|Configured static record slots and message size; no producer waits for device readiness.|The queued implementation preserves pending offsets but needs sustained concurrent transport evidence.
NXR-242|UART acceptance accounting|UART counters shall distinguish accepted, rejected-full, truncated, transmitted and failed records.|Saturate the queue and formatting limit; every producer attempt has one documented outcome and byte conservation reconciles.|Use known record lengths and controlled transport errors with counter boundary fixtures.|Saturating diagnostic counters; fixed queue capacity.|A hello demo cannot establish that overload and truncation are visible to callers.
NXR-243|UART boot and BSP contract|UART initialization shall declare device base, clock, reset, baud and ordered-MMIO guarantees before the service worker runs.|Misaligned base and wrong device profile are rejected; measured pin baud matches the configured tolerance.|Combine address-validation host fixtures with board clock and pin-loopback measurements.|No runtime driver discovery; one permanent owner per configured UART.|The current AXI TX adapter intentionally does not initialize clocks, baud or reset.
NXR-244|UART receiver overrun policy|A future UART receiver shall expose bounded storage, per-error counters and a documented record resynchronization rule.|Overflow during a stalled reader does not corrupt adjacent memory and subsequent delimiter-framed packets recover.|Inject framing errors, bursts beyond capacity and consumer pauses on real hardware.|Fixed RX ring and finite ISR work per entry; no heap.|The supplied AXI adapter is TX-only and must not be described as a complete RX or interrupt driver.
NXR-245|External interrupt acknowledgement|Peripheral IRQ qualification shall establish source identification, acknowledgement order and pending recheck without losing a concurrent event.|An event arriving during acknowledgement is eventually serviced once; spurious sources do not call unrelated handlers.|Use hardware-generated edge and level stimuli and an independent event count.|Fixed handler table; bounded per-entry service and declared nesting policy.|Current timer-only dispatch cannot establish a working UART or DMA external interrupt path.
NXR-246|Interrupt storm containment|The BSP shall contain a continuously asserted peripheral source without permanently starving the scheduling timer.|Hold a level source active; the declared mask or defer policy activates and timer progress remains observable.|Inject a stuck source while measuring timer and high-priority worker progress externally.|Finite events-per-entry budget and bounded deferred queue.|Acknowledging a misbehaving device indefinitely can turn bounded driver code into scheduler starvation.
NXR-247|GPIO ownership and register races|GPIO qualification shall prove pin ownership and correct atomic set-clear semantics under concurrent unrelated pin updates.|Two threads toggle disjoint pins without lost updates; conflicting ownership is rejected before register access.|Capture pins externally and exercise write-one and read-modify-write register variants.|Fixed pin table; critical section limit declared for non-atomic hardware.|Generic volatile access alone does not protect shared GPIO read-modify-write operations.
NXR-248|Watchdog progress quorum|Watchdog feeding shall require progress from every mandatory worker in the current supervision epoch.|Freeze one required worker while the monitor stays alive; feeding stops and the declared reset is observed.|Inject worker stalls at differing priorities and compare independent reset timing.|Fixed worker bitmap and finite epoch; optional workers explicitly excluded.|A heartbeat from one healthy monitor cannot establish overall scheduler or application progress.
NXR-249|SPI transaction cancellation|SPI cancellation shall retain chip select and buffer ownership until the controller is demonstrably quiescent.|Cancel mid-transfer and on completion boundary; no new client begins before settled bus ownership.|Observe chip select and clock while injecting completion delay and controller faults.|Fixed transaction slots and finite cancellation deadline; uncertain hardware quarantined.|A mutex around submission alone does not serialize the full hardware transaction lifetime.
NXR-250|I2C stuck-bus recovery evidence|I2C recovery shall use a board-declared bounded procedure and report failure when SCL or SDA cannot be released.|NACK, clock stretching and permanently low lines yield distinct terminal outcomes without indefinite polling.|Use programmable pull-down fixtures and capture recovery pulses and stop generation.|Finite stretch and recovery pulse limits; no unconditional bus-reset loops.|Recovery must be based on actual line state and board electrical constraints.
NXR-251|DMA late-completion isolation|DMA qualification shall reject a completion for an old ticket even after cancellation and a later transfer starts.|Replay old completion before and after new completion; only the current generation changes state or data.|Use an independently scripted backend and physical delayed IRQ where available.|One active ticket per channel; generations never wrap within object lifetime.|A device completion bit without transfer identity can corrupt the next owner's result.
NXR-252|DMA quiescence proof boundary|A DMA backend shall report completed or stopped only when no further bus writes from that operation can occur.|Delay the final write beyond the completion indication; qualification fails a backend that declares quiescence early.|Instrument bus writes or FPGA markers and compare final access against ownership release.|Finite drain deadline; uncertain stop enters quarantine.|The portable channel relies on backend quiescence guarantees that host state tests cannot prove.
NXR-253|DMA cache-line exclusivity|DMA buffers shall own all cache lines touched by prepare and finish maintenance operations.|Adjacent application canaries survive transmit and receive tests with unaligned requested lengths.|Exercise first and last cache-line boundaries on a cached board with independently observed buffer contents.|Staging alignment and rounded capacity cover the hardware cache-line size.|Correct byte bounds do not prevent cache maintenance from touching neighboring objects.
NXR-254|DMA failed-start side effects|A backend start failure shall guarantee that no transfer was issued and no application buffer remains hardware-owned.|A false start result followed by any bus activity fails; retry preserves channel ownership and counters.|Script rejected starts and monitor bus activity or FPGA transfer counters.|No application pointer retained; fixed channel staging allocation.|The interface's false-start guarantee is essential to safe immediate reuse.
NXR-255|DMA quarantine recovery domain|A quarantined channel shall remain unavailable until the declared reset domain proves hardware access has ended.|Repeated begin and cancel calls cannot release quarantined staging; reset of an unrelated peripheral does not recover it.|Inject stop failure, attempt reuse and verify only the qualified reset clears ownership.|Staging remains reserved during quarantine; no automatic generation reset.|Software state reset cannot stop a bus master that still owns memory.
NXR-256|No silent hardware skips|Qualification shall distinguish passed, failed, unsupported, not-run and incomplete for every required board-profile test.|Remove required UART checker or unavailable PMP support; aggregate release status cannot remain passed.|Validate result aggregation with mixed result fixtures and missing required evidence.|Finite declared test matrix; no implicit default-pass entries.|A green aggregate is misleading if unsupported hardware tests disappear from the denominator.
NXR-257|Evidence-qualified feature claims|Release notes shall link every supported feature and board claim to its configuration and retained acceptance evidence.|Delete or mismatch an evidence fingerprint and the corresponding qualified claim is rejected.|Cross-check release manifest, requirement IDs, board identities and evidence hashes.|One finite support matrix; planned work is never promoted by documentation generation.|Source presence and generated requirements are not sufficient evidence of target support.
NXR-258|Fair RTOS comparison profile|Any published NexRT comparison shall match workload semantics, optimization, allocation, clock and enabled services across compared systems.|An unmatched interrupt profile or omitted feature cost invalidates the comparison label.|Retain complete build inputs, measurement method and raw repeated samples for every participant.|Comparison scope and memory accounting fixed before measurements.|Small default images cannot fairly be compared with another RTOS configured for additional services.
NXR-259|Task requirement closure audit|Each qualification task shall retain a stable requirement link, specific acceptance criteria, dependency list and unresolved evidence status.|Duplicate IDs, missing acceptance, cyclic dependencies and stale generated task text fail the repository check.|Run negative tracker fixtures and tamper with one generated task to prove stale-output detection.|Exactly one task per imported qualification review; at most 100 in this campaign.|The earlier HIL design CSV was outside the canonical StrictDoc export and needs explicit implementation task traceability.
NXR-260|Ten-review milestone integrity|The campaign shall build and verify each ten-review boundary before making its local milestone commit.|Boundaries 10 through 100 each retain command logs, catalogue hash and successful exit codes; a failed command prevents commit.|Run the documented local checkpoint workflow and verify milestone ancestry without network operations.|Ten bounded milestones; no push; source and build evidence retained separately.|Counting authored rows is not equivalent to executing 100 runtime engineering changes and must remain labeled as requirements review.
